    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "The combinatorial effects of sequential neuroimmune insults have been implicated in the pathogenesis of many neurological disorders. To explore how prenatal and early postnatal innate immune activation impacts the developmental establishment of neural networks, we developed a neuron-astrocyte coculture model of virus-like neuroinflammatory challenges and used calcium imaging with the GCaMP reporter to document cell- and ensemble-level activity patterns over time. Neurons co-cultured on naïve astrocytes provided baseline measures of activity and synchrony within neuronal ensembles. Interferon gamma (IFNγ) exposure measurably accelerated the acquisition of synchrony within ensembles co-cultured with serum-preactivated, but not naïve, astrocytes. Machine learning analyses of neuronal synchrony data identified distinct, immune exposure-dependent patterns of maturation which are sufficient to predict the immune exposure history of cells based on their signaling properties with over 90% accuracy. Combined exposure to IFNγ and primed astrocytes was also correlated with increases in the ratio of protein expression levels of chloride symporters KCC2 and NKCC1, suggestive of accelerated neuronal maturation in dual hit-challenged cells. Collectively, these findings suggest that early-life inflammatory exposures can prime astrocytes to respond differently to later immune events than naïve astrocytes and identify one potential mechanism through which immune challenges can alter key neural network milestones in perinatal development.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
